      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What Sunday's application actually asks</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="The coverage's claim: the administration is asking the Court to let it enforce the March executive order for the midterms" variant="contested">
            <p>Too broad. Application 26A305 asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of named sections of the Postal Service's August 26 rule. The March order's own injunction was stayed on August 24, and that opinion said the stay did not decide whether later implementing measures would be lawful. The restriction now before the Court is the rule's acceptance screen, including the line that noncompliant mailings will not be accepted.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="APPLICATION FOR A STAY OF THE INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="will not be accepted" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The claim readers brought is the one in the candidate headlines: a new block, and another appeal. Both are true as dates. The new block is the September 4 injunction. The earlier appeal that "another" points to is 26A297 on Thursday, and behind that 26A124 in July.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /></p>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the day counts were made</summary>
          <p>November 3, 2026, minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToElection} days. Minus September 4 is {computed.daysPiToElection}. Minus September 3 is {computed.days297ToElection}. Minus August 26 is {computed.daysRuleToElection}. Minus March 31 is {computed.daysEoToElection}. September 19 minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToUocava}. November 3 minus September 19 is {computed.uocavaDaysBeforeElection}, the statutory 45-day overseas and military ballot date when a valid request arrived in time. August 26 minus August 24 is {computed.daysStayToRule}. Plaintiff states were counted from the parties list in 26A305: {computed.plaintiffStates} named states, plus the District of Columbia and the Pennsylvania governor, and {computed.intervenorStates} named intervenors.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">After Sunday</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 8 the 26A305 docket added amicus briefs from bipartisan current and former members of Congress and from the Society for the Rule of Law. The Congress brief argues that the order and final rule usurp Congress's constitutional role as the ultimate authority on federal-election administration. Jackson's response deadline remains 4 p.m. on September 9.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-congress-amicus" passage="THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: the September 9 responses, any administrative stay, and whether Alabama mails on the date Sauer named.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Alabama" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>Whether the Court will stay the September 4 injunction, and on what timetable. Jackson's order on the docket is the September 9 response deadline.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." /></li>
          <li>The First Circuit's ruling on the stay motions Sauer filed there. The application lists those motions as filed September 5. The Circuit's order is not in our records.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="(stay motion" /></li>
          <li>Which five states Sauer meant by "the week of September 13" in the Sunday application. The Thursday application named Indiana, Florida, Michigan, Nevada, and Wisconsin among "at least 16 more States" if the temporary order were extended.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-application" passage="including Indiana, Florida," /></li>
          <li>How many mail ballots each plaintiff state has already sent. Sauer names North Carolina as having begun; the application does not give a count.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /></li>
        </ul>
